{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080051,"nonce":"HIBQzhHE2UnFKg7MO3OgzobjFqE=","title":"`config.Default()` silently overrides six of the eight queue widths `DefaultQueueWorkers` publishes, so SPEC §G.3's table describes no process anybody runs","message":"**What is wrong.** `DefaultQueueWorkers()` (`internal/platform/jobs/client.go:38-50`) is documented as *\"the per-queue concurrency of SPEC §G.3\"* and it matches the SPEC table exactly: `ingest` 16, `enrich` 8, `notify` 8, `deliver_slack` 4, `deliver_webhook` 8, `reconcile` 8, `lifecycle` 4, `maintenance` 1 (`docs/design/SPEC.md:3378-3396`). It is then almost entirely discarded. `FromPlatformConfig` (`:104-120`) takes each `config.JobsConfig` field and applies it whenever it is `\u003e 0`, and `config.Default()` (`internal/platform/config/config.go:322-334`) ships every one of them non-zero: `QueueIngest: 10`, `QueueDefault: 5`, `QueueDelivery: 5`, `QueueReconcile: 8`. A default process therefore runs `ingest` 10, `enrich` 5, `notify` 5, `lifecycle` 5, `deliver_slack` 5, `deliver_webhook` 5, `reconcile` 8, `maintenance` 1 — **six of eight queues differ from the published number, and the map that names them is dead in every deployment that does not set the environment.**\n\n**The demonstration.** Read, not run, and it is four lines. `client.go:106` — `if cfg.QueueIngest \u003e 0 { queues[QueueIngest] = cfg.QueueIngest }` — with `config.go:324` — `QueueIngest: 10`. The `\u003e 0` test is what makes the default map unreachable: it was written so that an unset knob falls through to the SPEC number, and `config.Default()` never leaves one unset, so nothing falls through. `QueueDefault: 5` alone rewrites three queues (`:110-114`: `enrich`, `notify`, `lifecycle`) and `QueueDelivery: 5` rewrites two more (`:115-118`).\n\nTwo of the six move in the direction the code's own comment forbids. `client.go:25-27` says *\"`deliver_slack` is the narrowest because Slack allows roughly one message per second per channel, so extra workers buy contention and 429s rather than throughput\"* — and `QueueDelivery: 5` **widens** it from 4 to 5. `lifecycle` widens from 4 to 5 the same way. `ingest`, meanwhile, is described at `:24-25` as *\"the widest because a webhook batch must never queue behind anything\"*, and at 10 it is no longer the widest: `reconcile` at 8 and `deliver_webhook` at 5 are closer to it than the table intends, and the one queue SPEC §G.3.1 makes a BINDING capacity ceiling is the only one that survives intact — `QueueReconcile: 8` agrees with `DefaultQueueWorkers` because somebody checked that one.\n\n**Why it matters.** §G.3.1 publishes arithmetic — `2·N·S·t + 0.003·N ≤ 30·W` — and invites an operator to re-derive `W` against their own upstreams. That invitation is only honest if the `W` in the table is the `W` in the process. For `reconcile` it is; for every other row it is not, and an operator sizing `enrich` or reading \"16\" for `ingest` while their pods run 10 is debugging against a document that describes nothing. The failure is silent in both directions: no boot log names the divergence, `jobs.queue_ingest` is absent from no environment because it is never unset, and the SPEC table reads as a statement of fact rather than as one of two competing defaults.\n\nThe deeper problem is that there are two default tables at all. `DefaultQueueWorkers` carries the reasoning — why `deliver_slack` is 4, why `maintenance` is 1, why `reconcile` is 8 — and `config.Default()` carries the numbers that actually run, with reasoning for exactly one of them. Whichever is kept, one of them must stop being a default.\n\n**Where.** `internal/platform/jobs/client.go:22-50` (`DefaultQueueWorkers` and its rationale), `:104-120` (`FromPlatformConfig`, the six `\u003e 0` overrides) · `internal/platform/config/config.go:322-334` (`Jobs: JobsConfig{...}` in `Default()`), `:166-184` (the knob declarations) · `docs/design/SPEC.md:3378-3396` (the §G.3 table), `:3399-3420` (§G.3.1, the one row that agrees).\n\n**Scope.** small — the change is a handful of numbers — but it carries a decision that is not the implementer's to make by accident: whether `config.Default()`'s queue fields go to zero so the SPEC map is the default, or whether the SPEC table and `DefaultQueueWorkers` are amended to the shipped 10/5/5 and their rationale rewritten to match. The first is almost certainly right, because the rationale lives with the map; but it raises every default process's worker count and that is a capacity change.\n\n**Done when.** One table decides a default queue width, and reading it tells an operator what their process runs. If `DefaultQueueWorkers` is that table, `config.JobsConfig`'s queue fields default to zero and are overrides rather than defaults, and the `\u003e 0` fall-through does what it was written to do. If `config.Default()` is that table, SPEC §G.3, `DefaultQueueWorkers` and the `deliver_slack`-is-narrowest and `ingest`-is-widest rationale are all amended to the numbers that ship. Either way a test asserts that the widths `FromPlatformConfig(config.Default().Jobs)` produces are the widths SPEC §G.3 publishes, so the two cannot drift again — the same guard `defaults_derivation_test.go` already gives the org settings.","files":null}]}